   "id": "notes",
   "heading": "Notes",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "notes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "The model behind [IS-MP and Phillips Curve Diagram](note:is-mp-and-phillips-curve-diagram) and [Inflation Shock Paths](note:inflation-shock-paths). Place those views, not this note."
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Model.** [IS-MP-PC Model](note:is-mp-pc-model): Phillips curve $\\pi_t = \\pi^e_t + \\gamma(y_t - y^*) + \\epsilon^\\pi_t$ ([Phillips Curve](note:phillips-curve)), IS curve $y_t = y^* - \\alpha(i_t - \\pi_t - r^*) + \\epsilon^y_t$ ([IS Curve](note:is-curve)), rule $i_t = r^* + \\pi^* + \\beta_\\pi(\\pi_t - \\pi^*)$ ([Monetary Policy Rule](note:monetary-policy-rule)). The derived values are the closed-form solution with $\\theta = 1/(1 + \\alpha\\gamma(\\beta_\\pi - 1))$.\n- **Two variables.** `y` is output on a diagram's horizontal axis; [IS-MP and Phillips Curve Diagram](note:is-mp-and-phillips-curve-diagram) draws the curves as inflation at each output. `pprev` is last period's inflation: with [Adaptive Expectations](note:adaptive-expectations) it is this period's expected inflation, so `pi_ad`, `y_ad`, `i_ad`, `r_ad` solve a period from the one before, which is what [Inflation Shock Paths](note:inflation-shock-paths) iterates."
      }
     ],
     "words": 103,
